c(25, "§5.1 table (verbatim); §5.1; §5.2 table (verbatim); §5.2 every-paid-reviewer table (verbatim); §5.2; §5.7", "monetization", "The monetisation model — one product, Premium one-time ($4.99 / ¥780 now; $3.99 in 2024–2025 archives), no subscription; from about January 2024 a free period of days to weeks after which the app cannot be used without Premium; before that freemium (two free calendars, ads, dark mode paid) (verbatim): " + table("## 5.1 The monetisation model", 0) + " — PAID_EXPLICIT 31 (12.25%), 15 of 31 (48.4%) with a post-purchase problem, 15 praise the price without saying they paid, 1 refund request, 1 disputed charge, lost_user_signal 27; codes (verbatim): " + table("## 5.2 Every paid reviewer", 0) + " || every paid reviewer (verbatim): " + table("## 5.2 Every paid reviewer", 1) + " — paid reviewers by storefront: Japan 11, Brazil 5, US 5, Colombia 2, Australia 2, one each in Poland, Estonia, Egypt, Canada, China and Uruguay; no conversion, refund or retention rate can be derived; no price elasticity (reviewers quote $3.99 to $6 and 600–800円; no one compares before and after a change); 27 lost-user statements are a floor on those who wrote, not those who left.", "one-time Premium; 31 explicit buyers", "mixed", "PAID 31 / 12.25% / 3.48★; paid_problem 15 of 31; RESTORE 9; DATA_LOSS 6; DISPUTE 2; ADS_AFTER_PURCHASE 2; LOCKIN 2; jp buyers 11", "none", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["10628286567", "10355887642", "8669998205", "8943842333", "10307647024", "10629798692", "11046112570", "12171082510", "13599884686", "14195716095"], "", "")

c(26, "§5.3 table (verbatim); §5.3", "data-caveat", "Segment rates among the 31 buyers vs 222 non-buyers (verbatim): " + table("## 5.3 Segment rates", 0) + " — paid_problem 48.4% vs 0.5%; restore failure 29.0% vs 0; data loss 16.1% vs 0.5%; buyers carry less paywall complaint (25.8% vs 44.6%) and slightly less specific praise (32.3% vs 39.6%); REPORT_ALL_MARKS_COUNTED 6.5% of buyers vs 0.9%; 'segment rates (denominator 31), shown beside the global count; they are not threshold classifications'.", "n/a", "mixed", "paid_problem 48.4% vs 0.5%; RESTORE 29.0%; DATA_LOSS 16.1%; paywall 25.8% vs 44.6%", "none", "table", "generalisable", ["8943842333", "11804387968"], "", "")

c(27, "§5.4 table (verbatim); §5.4; §5.5", "insight", "What triggers a purchase among the 31 (verbatim): " + table("## 5.4 What triggers a purchase", 0) + " — liked it immediately and the price is small ('As soon as I opened I liked it and got the premium version'; 'I immediately paid for the premium version. Guys, it's only $3.99'); the model is one-time; backup / keeping records safe ('so they store my data and i don't loose it'; 'para ter o backup automático' — both then lost their data); records already entered (PURCHASE_LOCKIN 2: 'didn't want to pay, but had already entered records, so bought', jp, translated); a specific use (piano practice, work attendance, diet then exercise); 'two of the stated reasons to buy — keeping data safe and not losing records already entered — are the same things that failed for other buyers and that the trial wall threatens for non-buyers'. What goes wrong after purchase: device_change_fail 12 (4.74%) — 9 restore failures (all paid) and 6 data-loss reports (5 paid), consistent 2022 → 2025: new phone → purchase not recognised or records empty → asked to pay again ('I already PAID and now it wants me to pay again???'); ads after purchase 2 (2022, 2023); counting — 2 of the 4 ALL_MARKS_COUNTED reviewers are paid; timing: 7 of 12 E1 buyers report a problem, 4 of 6 in 2024, 4 of 8 in 2025, 0 of 5 in 2026.", "buy for backup → backup fails on the new phone", "purchase-driver", "triggers: immediate like + small price 4; one-time 2; backup 2 (both lost data); lock-in 2; specific use 3; device_change_fail 12 / 4.74%; buyer problem by era 7/12, 4/6, 4/8, 0/5", "must-never-break", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["10517465781", "10629798692", "13033057057", "12602169408", "10498260582", "13599884686", "11804387968", "13098699650", "11046112570", "12775365144", "13786337570", "13584852727", "14195716095", "12171082510"], "records already entered convert a reluctant user into a buyer — the same records the wall locks for non-buyers", "")

c(28, "§5.6 table (verbatim); §5.6", "monetization", "Upgrade barriers among non-buyers (verbatim): " + table("## 5.6 Upgrade barriers", 0) + " — the corpus contains no one who says the price is too high and that they would pay a lower one, except one Russian reviewer ('a couple of hundred roubles'); 'the objections are to how the wall arrives, to paying for something basic at all, and to losing records'.", "undisclosed wall; too-simple objection; locked records", "blocked-conversion", "UNDISCLOSED 27; NOT_WORTH 28; DATA_LOCKED 8; FREE_ALTERNATIVE 9; SUBSCRIPTION_BELIEF 5; PREFER_ADS 2; PLATFORM_GAP 2; lower-price ask 1", "product-rule", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["12163557097", "14109888309", "14385347572", "11478965968", "13718504025", "14381263188", "11513669707", "12179154565", "13657817683", "14362887124", "11282003485", "10818815408", "13123238983", "13689050772", "12716103218"], "", "")

c(29, "§6.1 table (verbatim); §6.1; §6.2; §6.8", "market", "Full storefront table (verbatim): " + table("## 6.1 The full storefront table", 0) + " — only the US (61) and Japan (60) reach 50 (together 47.83%); next Brazil 19, India 12, Canada 10, Mexico 10; 16 storefronts have exactly one review; corpus 3.332★ vs 4.676★ across 7,065 public ratings (+1.344★); the gap is largest where the paywall share is highest (Canada +2.82, Vietnam +2.78, Chile +3.74 on 2 reviews, Brazil +1.65, Japan +1.47, US +1.38) and smallest where it is lowest (India +0.55, China +0.58, UK +0.19) — 'the public rating is not a summary of the written reviews for this app, and the written reviews are not a summary of users'.", "n/a", "mixed", "us 61 / 3.36★ / +1.38; jp 60 / 3.05★ / +1.47; ca 10 / 1.80★ / +2.82; gap tracks paywall share", "none", "table", "generalisable", ["11513669707"], "", "")

c(30, "§6.3 table (verbatim); §6.3; §6.3 US-over-time table (verbatim)", "market", "United States — 61 (24.11%), 3.36★ (verbatim): " + table("## 6.3 United States", 0) + " — the same paywall share as everywhere but more accusatory: paywall_any 27 of 61 (44.3%) vs 41.7%; non-disclosure 11 of 61 (18.0%) and fraud language 7 of 61 (11.5%) — about double the rest ('App baits you into thinking it's free'; 'textbook bait and switch tactic'); loss stated more often (lost_user 14.8% vs 9.4%); fit is the praise (NEED_FIT 18.0% vs 10.4%; simplicity lower 21.3% vs 35.4% — 'US reviewers say does what I need more than simple'); more developer praise (16.4%) and no non-payment defects (0 of 61); fewer buyers (5 of 61, 8.2%); public 4.74★ over 1,492 — 1★ 3.28% public vs 27.87% text; over time (verbatim): " + table("## 6.3 United States", 1) + " — the US trial-wall share rises after 2024 (4 of 19 → 9 of 20 → 7 of 18); small per-era counts, an observation.", "n/a", "1★-burst", "us n=61: paywall 44.3%; UNDISCLOSED 18.0%; SCAM 11.5%; NEED_FIT 18.0%; defect 0; PAID 8.2%; trial-wall by era 0/4, 4/19, 9/20, 7/18", "none", "High-priority", "app-specific", ["13953491590", "13086753669", "10882459680"], "", "")

c(31, "§6.4 table (verbatim); §6.4; §6.4 JP-over-time table (verbatim)", "market", "Japan — 60 (23.72%), 3.05★ (verbatim): " + table("## 6.4 Japan", 0) + " — the highest paywall share (30 of 60, 50.0%) and the lowest mean, but fraud language rare (2 of 60): Japanese reviewers describe the trial precisely and ask for disclosure politely ('I think that kind of thing should be stated at the start…!', translated); price named and discussed (PRICE_NAMED 18.3% vs 4.7%, almost always 600円); conditional willingness 5 of 60 vs 1 of 193; more buyers (11 of 60, 18.3%); the most specific praise (praise_specific 51.7%, simplicity 48.3%) and only 1 of 60 uses the generic 'exactly what I needed'; health use 11.7% vs 3.6%; record defects are Japanese (defect_non_payment 9 of 60, 15.0% vs 2.6%); all 3 DEV_PROMISE_UNKEPT are Japanese — the storefront where the developer replied in writing; helpful votes concentrate here (17 of 21 voted reviews, 19 of 21 carry a paywall code; the most-voted review, 6 votes, is titled 'people who don't want to pay shouldn't install it'); public 4.52★ over 1,646 — 1★ 4.13% vs 30.00%; over time (verbatim): " + table("## 6.4 Japan", 1) + " — Japan is where the trial wall first landed: 11 of 21 Japanese 2024 reviews describe it; mean fell from 4.00★ (11 reviews before 2024) to 2.76★ in 2024; trial-wall share since fell (6 of 13, 4 of 15) while specific praise also fell (7 of 11 → 5 of 15).", "n/a", "mixed", "jp n=60: paywall 50.0%; SCAM 3.3%; PRICE_NAMED 18.3%; CONDITIONAL 8.3%; PAID 18.3%; praise_specific 51.7%; defect 15.0%; votes 17 of 21; mean 4.00 → 2.76 in 2024", "none", "High-priority", "app-specific", ["11777550164", "11046112570", "11511386286", "13293180955"], "", "")

c(32, "§6.5 table (verbatim); §6.5", "market", "High-spend (defined list, 11 present, US 61 of 99) vs not vs high-review-volume (us, jp, br, in, ca, mx) vs the 34 storefronts under 10 (verbatim): " + table("## 6.5 High-spend markets", 0) + " — high-review-volume storefronts carry the paywall problem (46.5% vs 33.3%; non-disclosure 14.0% vs 3.7%; mean 3.174★ vs 3.667★); smaller storefronts ask for more (requests 23.5% vs 11.6%); high-spend storefronts buy less (8.1% vs 14.9% — Japan and Brazil are outside the list) and object to price the same (13.1% vs 13.0%); non-payment defects almost absent from high-spend storefronts (1.0%) — a Japanese pattern.", "n/a", "mixed", "high-spend 99 / 3.253★ / paywall 46.5% / PAID 8.1%; not 154 / PAID 14.9%; ≥10 stores 172 / paywall 46.5%; <10 stores 81 / 33.3% / requests 23.5%", "research", "limited evidence", "generalisable", ["13315450144"], "", "")

c(33, "§6.6 table (verbatim); §6.6; §6.7 table (verbatim); §6.7", "market", "Below-50 storefronts, limited evidence (verbatim): " + table("## 6.6 Storefronts below 50", 0) + " — Canada 10 with 8 one-star and 8 paywall is the most negative storefront with ≥10 reviews; Brazil 19 with 5 buyers, two with device-change problems; India mostly short positive; Mexico framed as too simple to pay for; Vietnam all 4 paywall. Languages (verbatim): " + table("## 6.7 Storefront is not language", 0) + " — English is spread across 30 storefronts; Japanese confined to Japan (59 of 60); Spanish spans 7 storefronts including one review each on the US and Canadian storefronts; the one Arabic-language request is written in English on the US storefront and Arabic is not among the listing's 24 languages.", "n/a", "mixed", "ca 10 / 1.80★ / 8 paywall; br 19 / 5 buyers; in 12 / 4.08★; vn 4 / all paywall; en 127 (50.20%) in 30 stores; ja 59; es 23 in 7 stores", "none", "limited evidence", "generalisable", ["11513669707", "11756829140", "13884081040", "10789194783", "13098699650", "12183886855", "13157323803", "14362887124", "13315450144", "14381263188", "7677254284", "12339505669", "11950112016", "12104603863", "10985800910", "12541432220", "13946969862", "11450294263", "14119601087"], "", "")

c(34, "§7.1 era table (verbatim); §7.1 year table (verbatim); §7.1; §7.2", "timeline", "Eras defined by the one change the corpus itself dates — the first trial-wall review 2024-01-06 — then calendar year (verbatim): " + table("## 7.1 Method", 0) + " || by year (verbatim): " + table("## 7.1 Method", 1) + " — E1 freemium (36 reviews over 984 days, 1.10 per 30 days, 3.78★), E2 trial wall appears (67, 5.57 per 30 days, 2.91★), E3 (81, 3.48★), E4 (69, 8.35 per 30 days, 3.33★); the trial wall arrives and review volume jumps with it: 2024-Q1 holds 21 reviews, 11 describing the wall; the three busiest days of 2024 are its first week (3 each on 01-06, 01-10, 01-13); the first trial-wall review was written by someone who had used it about two months — existing users were caught by it, not only new installs; mean 3.78★ → 2.91★, one-star share 13.9% → 31.3%; paywall share quintuples (11.1% → 56.7%; TRIAL_WALL 0 → 46.3%) — 'timing, not proven cause — but no other theme moves at the same time on this scale'.", "trial wall applied to existing free users", "1★-burst", "E1 36 / 3.78★ / paywall 11.1%; E2 67 / 2.91★ / 56.7%; E3 81 / 3.48★; E4 69 / 3.33★; volume 1.10 → 5.57 → 6.66 → 8.35 per 30 days", "none", "table", "app-specific", ["10792547110"], "", "")

c(35, "§7.3 table (verbatim); §7.3 movement table (verbatim); §7.4; §7.5", "timeline", "Theme movement (verbatim): " + table("## 7.3 Theme movement", 0) + " || movements (verbatim): " + table("## 7.3 Theme movement", 1) + " — what improved: post-purchase failures stopped being reported (paid_problem 7 → 4 → 5 → 0; device_change_fail 3 → 4 → 5 → 0; last restore failure 2025-04-29, last data loss 2025-09-04; the listing now promises restore 'even across different devices'; with 5 buyers in 2026 a small base); locked records fell to 1 in 2026 (one 2025-01-15 reviewer said an update made old records visible; the one 2026 case still could not read notes); widgets and notifications shipped — five requests each until 2025, none since. What did not improve: disclosure — after the 2024-12-18 apology 18 of 151 later reviews still report non-disclosure and 42 the wall; after 2025-02-10, 15 of 139 and 37 of 139; the listing still does not mention a trial; the core complaint in 2026 is unchanged from 2024 ('I had no idea this was a paid app after a week'; 'Makes you pay $5 after a week').", "n/a", "mixed", "TRIAL_WALL 0 → 46.3 → 28.4 → 27.5%; UNDISCLOSED 0 → 13.4 → 13.6 → 10.1%; paid_problem 19.4 → 6.0 → 6.2 → 0%; ADS 8.3 → 3.0 → 0 → 0%; requests 25.0 → 5.8%; LOW_INFO 27.8 → 44.9%", "none", "High-priority", "app-specific", ["12186143823", "13864528351", "14109888309", "14385347572"], "", "")

c(36, "§7.6; §7.7; §9.H", "timeline", "What emerged in 2026: the Android price comparison (2, both January), building a replacement with AI (one review, 2026-07-29), record-integrity defects in Japan (counts not adding up 2025-11 and 2026-03, checks vanishing 2026-07), a complaint about daily review requests (2026-09-05), and more very short reviews (44.9% in E4) coinciding with a large public rating base (US ratings 258 in May 2024 → 868 July 2025 → 1,492 September 2026) — consistent with prompted reviewing, which cannot be tested here. Prices quoted over time (PRICE_NAMED 20): 600円 from 2024-01 to 2026-03, then 'it used to be 600 yen, now 800' on 2026-05-27 against ¥780 listed; in dollars $3.99 (2023-11), $4 (2025-03), $6 (AU 2025-03), 7 dollars (CA 2026-03), 6 dollars (2026-07), $5 (2026-08) against archived $3.99 (2024-05, 2025-07) and $4.99 now — consistent with a price increase between July 2025 and mid-2026 in both yen and dollars; the listing does not date it. Burst check: max 3 reviews on a day, three of those days in the first week of the trial wall — 'an event response across storefronts, not a coordinated burst'; 'Not free' ×5 titles all independent; no incentivised-review language.", "n/a", "mixed", "PLATFORM_GAP 2; AI replacement 1; REVIEW_PROMPT_EXCESS 1; LOW_INFO E4 44.9%; price 600 → 800円, $3.99 → $4.99; max 3/day", "none", "limited evidence", "app-specific", ["13689050772", "14362887124", "13410320941", "13800362190", "14270574717", "14513815810", "14112073954"], "", "")

c(37, "§8.1", "product-rule", "§8.1 immediate fix — disclose the trial before install and before the first check: TRIAL_WALL 73 (28.85%), UNDISCLOSED 27 (10.67%), trust_breach 42, 64 of 71 one-star carry a paywall code, the listing description (current and archived) never mentions a trial, the developer promised in 2024-12 and 2025-02 to make it clearer and 15 non-disclosure reviews followed the second promise; put the trial length and the one-time price in the first lines of the store description and on the first screen after install; show a visible countdown ('5 days left of your free trial, then Premium $4.99 once') from day one and a reminder the day before it ends; say 'one-time, not a subscription' wherever the price appears (SUBSCRIPTION_BELIEF 5); 'why first: it addresses the largest negative theme with text changes, and reviewers say it would have made them buyers'.", "undisclosed trial", "blocked-conversion", "TRIAL_WALL 73; UNDISCLOSED 27; trust_breach 42; 1★ paywall 64 of 71; SUBSCRIPTION_BELIEF 5", "product-rule", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["10882459680", "11511386286", "12775365144"], "", "")

c(38, "§8.2", "must-never-break", "§8.2 immediate fix — never lock people out of their own records: DATA_LOCKED 8 (3.16%), 'Holds your data hostage for money', USE_HEALTH 14 (medication), records are a stated reason to buy, one reviewer said locked records were fixed in January 2025 and another reported them locked in March 2026; after the trial keep history readable and CSV export available (the export already exists) even if new checks require Premium; verify the current build against the 2026-03-19 report.", "records unreadable after trial", "1★-burst", "DATA_LOCKED 8 / 3.16%; USE_HEALTH 14; lock-in buyers 2", "must-never-break", "Very strong", "generalisable", ["12179154565", "11046112570", "11804387968", "13864528351"], "", "")

c(39, "§8.3", "must-never-break", "§8.3 immediate fix — confirm restore and device migration for buyers: 15 of 31 buyers (48.4%) report a post-purchase problem, RESTORE_PURCHASE 9 (all buyers), DATA_LOSS 6, none reported in 2026 (5 buyers); treat the 2026 silence as unconfirmed — test new-device restore of both the purchase and the records, iPhone → iPhone and iPhone → iPad, make Restore purchase and backup status visible in settings, and give support a reachable path (SUPPORT_UNRESPONSIVE 2).", "restore silently fails on device change; no support path", "churn", "paid_problem 15 of 31; RESTORE 9; DATA_LOSS 6; 2026 0 of 5; UNRESPONSIVE 2", "must-never-break", "Very strong", "generalisable", ["11126142010", "10465840832", "12486925913"], "", "")

c(40, "§8.4", "tactic", "§8.4 experiment — a usable free tier instead of a hard wall: NOT_WORTH 28 (11.07%) object to charging for a basic calendar, FREE_ALTERNATIVE 9, two ask for ads instead of payment and two report an ad-supported option that could not be used, three use the free version; before 2024 the app was freemium (two calendars free) and paywall complaints were 11.1% of reviews against 42.0–56.7% in each year since; A/B an ongoing free tier (one or two calendars, or ads) against the current wall, measuring purchases, uninstalls and the review stream for NOT_WORTH, TRIAL_WALL and WARN_OTHERS text — 'the corpus cannot say which converts better; it says which one reviewers resent'.", "hard wall replaced a freemium tier", "mixed", "NOT_WORTH 28; FREE_ALTERNATIVE 9; PREFER_ADS 2; FREE_OPTION_BLOCKED 2; FREE_TIER_OK 3; paywall E1 11.1% vs 42.0–56.7% after", "tactic", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["10818815408", "13123238983", "11511386286", "14218270943", "10790606920"], "", "")

c(41, "§8.5", "tactic", "§8.5 experiment — sell Premium on what buyers name, not on basic ticking: buyers name the one-time price (PAY_ONCE_PRAISE 10), keeping records safe, and specific uses; paid-extra willingness predates the wall (2021: reminders, icons, colours — 'even as a paid option', jp, translated); widgets were a stated purchase condition; test positioning Premium around backup/sync across devices, unlimited calendars, per-emoji reports and widgets, with basic ticking free.", "premium gates the core tick", "purchase-driver", "PAY_ONCE 10; paid-extra willingness 2021; widget condition 1", "tactic", "Very strong", "generalisable", ["7684285778", "10792547110", "11804387968", "13644913370"], "", "")

c(42, "§8.6", "must-have", "§8.6 immediate fix — make counts match what users marked: REPORT_ALL_MARKS_COUNTED 4 (1.58%) — a ❌ day counts as done (one 1★, two buyers); DEF_COUNT_WRONG 2 and DEF_CHECK_LOST 1, all Japanese, 2025-11 → 2026-07; count per emoji/mark in reports; reproduce the two 'total doesn't match' reports and the 'checks vanish after reopening' report — 'for a record-keeping app these defects undermine the reason to pay'.", "reports count ❌ as done", "complaint", "ALL_MARKS_COUNTED 4; COUNT_WRONG 2; CHECK_LOST 1", "must-have", "Meaningful", "generalisable", ["10775717295", "12710720903", "12787415461", "13644913370", "13410320941", "13800362190", "14270574717"], "", "")

c(43, "§8.7; §8.8", "insight", "§8.7 research — how is the review prompt used and what does it do to the rating: corpus 3.332★ vs public 4.676★ over 7,065, seven five-star reviews with negative text, very short reviews rising to 44.9% in 2026, one complaint of daily review requests; how often does the prompt fire and at what point in the trial — if before the trial ends, the public rating mostly reflects the pre-wall experience; store rules on prompt frequency should be checked. §8.8 research — reconcile the privacy statements: 'never sent to third parties' vs 'Data Used to Track You: Diagnostics' vs a policy that sends edited data 'to the analysis service'; one reviewer objects to cloud backup; nothing in the corpus shows harm — 'the risk is to a positioning a reviewer already questions'.", "n/a", "mixed", "gap +1.344★; 5★-negative 7; LOW_INFO E4 44.9%; PROMPT_EXCESS 1; PRIV 1", "research", "Emerging", "generalisable", ["14513815810", "13353478605", "11449760821"], "", "")

c(44, "§8.9 table (verbatim); §8.9", "dont", "§8.9 what this corpus says not to prioritise (verbatim): " + table("## 8.9 What this corpus says", 0) + " — widgets or notifications (already in the listing; no request since 2025-08-23 apart from one 2026 multi-habit widget request); a stability overhaul (zero crash or freeze reports; one 'wouldn't open' in five years); more features for their own sake (simplicity is the praise; reviewers cite other apps' clutter); a subscription (none exists; believers rate it 2.20★; one-time is praised); country-specific pricing (objections are about paying at all, not the amount); social / sharing (no request in 253 reviews).", "n/a", "mixed", "6 don'ts", "dont", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["14327295455", "12486925913", "12787415461", "11282003485"], "", "")

c(45, "§9.I table (verbatim); §9.I; §3.6", "do", "Developer responses on the listings [external] (verbatim): " + table("## 9.I Developer responses", 0) + " — the developer replies publicly on both storefronts: explains the one-tap design rationale, apologises twice for the pricing mechanism (2024-12-18, 2025-02-10), corrects the subscription belief ('a one-time payment, not a monthly fee'), acknowledges repeat-task and cross-device sync requests as under consideration, and confirms a shipped graph-period request; the apologies were not followed by a listing change (§7.5), and the three unkept-promise reviews all sit on the storefront where they were made.", "public replies; listing unchanged", "mixed", "7 replies; 2 apologies; PROMISE_UNKEPT 3 all jp", "do", "limited evidence", "generalisable", ["11171003334", "11511386286", "11777550164", "11046112570", "11282003485", "11129150989", "11592600279", "12398233280"], "a public apology that is not followed by the fix is quoted back as evidence of bad faith", "")

